1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,167
Where does a fraction go when we write it

2
00:00:02,167 --> 00:00:06,962
as a decimal? Decimal Forms. Take five-eighths, and three-sevenths.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:10,115
A fraction can always become a decimal. What decimal

4
00:00:10,115 --> 00:00:11,691
does a fraction become?

5
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:15,530
Here's the key idea: a fraction is really just

6
00:00:15,530 --> 00:00:18,923
a division. P over Q simply means P divided

7
00:00:18,923 --> 00:00:23,013
by Q. Take five-eighths — that becomes five divided

8
00:00:23,013 --> 00:00:26,754
by eight. To get the decimal form, divide the

9
00:00:26,754 --> 00:00:31,104
numerator by the denominator. Of course, Q can never

10
00:00:31,104 --> 00:00:32,061
be zero.

11
00:00:32,417 --> 00:00:35,286
Let's actually do the division for five divided by

12
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:38,429
eight. Set it up and start dividing. The first

13
00:00:38,429 --> 00:00:41,435
digit is six, leaving a remainder of two. Keep

14
00:00:41,435 --> 00:00:44,509
going: the next digit is two, leaving a remainder

15
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:47,242
of four. One more step gives five, and now

16
00:00:47,242 --> 00:00:50,999
the remainder becomes zero! When the remainder hits zero,

17
00:00:50,999 --> 00:00:54,757
the decimal simply stops. So five-eighths equals zero point

18
00:00:54,757 --> 00:00:57,148
six two five — a terminating decimal.

19
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,884
Now let's try three divided by seven. Setting up

20
00:01:00,884 --> 00:01:04,500
the division. Watch the digits appear one by one,

21
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,961
while we track the remainder at every step. After

22
00:01:07,961 --> 00:01:11,422
six steps, look closely — the remainder three shows

23
00:01:11,422 --> 00:01:15,422
up again! Since the remainder repeats, the digits must

24
00:01:15,422 --> 00:01:19,499
repeat too: three-sevenths equals zero point four two eight

25
00:01:19,499 --> 00:01:23,960
five seven one, repeating forever. The digits repeat forever

26
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,191
— this is a recurring decimal.

27
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:29,135
So why do the digits repeat? The secret is

28
00:01:29,135 --> 00:01:32,537
the remainder. Follow the chain of remainders from our

29
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:37,027
division: three, two, six, four, five, one. Each remainder

30
00:01:37,027 --> 00:01:40,157
leads to the next through the same division steps.

31
00:01:40,157 --> 00:01:43,694
And eventually, the very first remainder shows up again,

32
00:01:43,694 --> 00:01:47,232
closing the loop. The same remainder always produces the

33
00:01:47,232 --> 00:01:51,246
same division steps, and therefore the same repeating digits.

34
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:54,784
For terminating decimals, the remainder reaches zero — for

35
00:01:54,784 --> 00:01:57,641
recurring decimals, a remainder repeats.

36
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,051
Let's place both side by side. On the left,

37
00:02:01,051 --> 00:02:04,102
five-eighths equals zero point six two five — the

38
00:02:04,102 --> 00:02:07,896
remainder reached zero, so the division stops. On the

39
00:02:07,896 --> 00:02:11,840
right, three-sevenths equals zero point four two eight five

40
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:15,114
seven one repeating — a remainder repeated, so the

41
00:02:15,114 --> 00:02:20,100
division loops forever. Remember it simply: stop means terminating,

42
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:21,811
loop means recurring.

43
00:02:22,167 --> 00:02:25,160
Time to predict the decimal type for two fractions.

44
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,663
The first fraction is three-fourths. The second fraction is

45
00:02:28,663 --> 00:02:32,166
two-sevenths. Take a moment here, and watch the remainder

46
00:02:32,166 --> 00:02:33,058
carefully.

47
00:02:43,867 --> 00:02:47,581
Three divided by four gives zero point seven five,

48
00:02:47,581 --> 00:02:50,620
and the remainder becomes zero — so that one

49
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:54,756
is terminating. Two divided by seven gives zero point

50
00:02:54,756 --> 00:02:58,301
two eight five seven one four, and the remainder

51
00:02:58,301 --> 00:03:01,678
keeps repeating — so that one is recurring.

52
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:05,782
Let's lock in the big idea. Every fraction becomes

53
00:03:05,782 --> 00:03:09,555
a decimal through division. If the remainder reaches zero,

54
00:03:09,555 --> 00:03:13,260
the decimal terminates — like five-eighths, which equals zero

55
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:16,291
point six two five. If a remainder repeats, the

56
00:03:16,291 --> 00:03:19,996
decimal recurs — like three-sevenths, which equals zero point

57
00:03:19,996 --> 00:03:23,836
four two eight five seven one, repeating forever. Divide,

58
00:03:23,836 --> 00:03:27,271
watch the remainder, and you'll always know: stop, or

59
00:03:27,271 --> 00:03:30,033
loop. Thank you so much for watching, and I

60
00:03:30,033 --> 00:03:32,054
will see you in the next lesson!
